{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Deeper than the skin of his flesh.</b> Every white appearance is deep, like the appearance of sunlight which is deeper than shade — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 13:3). And because of this, when the Rav reached the second verse, which says, \"And if it is a bright white spot, and it is not deeper than the skin\" (Leviticus 13:4), the Rav wrote: I do not know its meaning. The explanation was difficult for him, since it is a bright white spot (בהרת לבנה) — it is impossible that its appearance should not be deep, like the appearance of sunlight which is deeper than shade.\n\nNow, we can resolve this difficulty by saying that the verses do not state of the appearance of a nega that it is \"deeper than the skin\" except when it turns white hair in it; but when it says, \"and the hair in it has not turned white\" (Leviticus 13:4), it then says, \"and it is not deeper than the skin,\" for even the appearance of sunlight — if there were something black scattered in that place, the appearance would not look deep to one gazing at it. And hair in its natural state is black and nullifies the depth of the nega; but when it turns white or yellow, the whiteness will all be radiant and will appear deep to every observer from a distance.\n\nNevertheless, what the Rav said, that \"every white appearance is deep,\" is not correct in my eyes, for they said (Shevuot 6b): there is no שאת (se'et) except an expression of elevation (גבוה), and the se'et is white, as it is written (Leviticus 13:10), \"a white se'et (שאת לבנה)\" — and they said (Negaim 1:1): se'et is like white wool, its counterpart is like the membrane of an egg — and it is very white and ought to be deep, so why do they call it elevated (גבוה)? And Scripture also does not say of the appearance of the se'et, \"deeper than the skin.\" And in Torat Kohanim (Sifra, Negaim, parasha 1:4) they said: what does \"se'et\" mean? Elevated (מוגבהת), like the appearance of shade, which is higher than the appearance of sunlight. And if every white appearance were deep, behold the matter is the opposite.\n\nPerhaps we may say that the expression \"se'et\" — elevated — is used in contrast to the baheret: if you examine both of them, the baheret would be like the appearance of sunlight and the se'et at its side like the appearance of shade, so that relative to the skin, both are deep; but Scripture did not say of the appearance of the se'et, \"deeper than the skin.\"\n\nBut this is how the matter appears to me: there is a white that gives a brilliance and glare to the eyes like the appearance of the sun, and the eye does not absorb that white color, and therefore it appears to him as deep — like the appearance of sunlight, which is deeper than shade — because the eye absorbs the black color and it is fixed in it, while the white color disperses the power of sight and pushes it away, and therefore it appears deep. Now the whiteness of the baheret, which is bright and intense as snow — the sight is weakened by it just as it is weakened in the place of the sun, provided there is no black hair in it, for then the power of sight grasps the blackness and from it extends over the entire appearance of the nega, and does not flee from it. But the se'et is white, yet the white does not radiate and does not weaken the sight; rather, sight extends and draws near to it, and it appears close to him and elevated — like the phenomenon of sight regarding the stars, which appear as though high in the firmament.\n\nAnd Scripture mentioned concerning the boil (Leviticus 13:19) two appearances: a white se'et or a baheret tinged with reddish, and it said of it (Leviticus 13:20), \"its appearance is lower (שפל)\" — not deep — because that baheret, even though it is intensely white, the reddish within it diminishes from it the depth, and it reverts somewhat to lowness. And according to the plain sense, \"and behold, its appearance is lower than the skin\" (ibid.) refers only to the baheret, but of a white se'et one would not say so. And it said of both of them (Leviticus 13:21): \"And if there is no white hair in it, and it is not lower than the skin, and it is dim (כהה)\" — because since it has the reddish appearance, and the blackness of the hair is lost from it, also the lowness — and it is dim. And it mentioned concerning the burn (Leviticus 13:24): \"white, reddish, or white,\" and it said (Leviticus 13:25): \"its appearance is deep\" — regarding the white one. And it went back and said (Leviticus 13:26): \"And behold, there is no white hair in the baheret, and it is not lower than the skin\" — because it mentioned the matter of lowness regarding the white-reddish one, to teach that the deep and the low are both signs of impurity, and there is no purity for them except when there is no lowness at all and it is dim (כהה).\n\nAnd the Torah desired the purity of Israel and the cleanliness of their bodies, and distanced this disease from its inception, for these appearances are not yet full-blown tzara'at, but will come to that. And the physicians say in their books: blemishes — one fears from them the onset of tzara'at. Therefore Scripture says of them at their inception (here, Leviticus 13:3): \"the plague of tzara'at (נגע צרעת)\" — meaning, a lesion of tzara'at — it is not full-blown tzara'at; and when the signs of impurity are complete after the isolation, and it says, \"it is tzara'at\" (Leviticus 13:8), it is possible that it is full-blown tzara'at. And sometimes it says regarding impurity (Leviticus 13:20): \"And the priest shall pronounce him impure; it is the plague of tzara'at,\" and the intent is to say that he is to be pronounced impure by it from now, for it is a plague that will certainly come to tzara'at, and it is fitting to separate him from now from the people. And likewise, \"And the priest shall pronounce him impure; it is a plague\" (Leviticus 13:22) — it means that it is a great plague that will not heal but will grow all its days and spread as it has spread now.\n\nAnd as for what Rashi said (Rashi on Leviticus 13:3) concerning white hair, that it is a sign of impurity and it is a decree of Scripture — this is the explanation of the matter, and it is a supernal decree that comes upon that man. For the nega that does not turn the hair white is only a blemish in the skin, not a lesion that makes sick at all."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "The Rav (הרב) refers to Maimonides, who wrote 'I do not know its meaning' on Leviticus 13:4. Ramban quotes Aramaic phrases (דנא פשרה דמלתא וגזרת עלאה היא די מטא על גברא ההוא) near the end, rendered literally: 'this is the explanation of the matter, and it is a supernal decree that comes upon that man.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:53:44.485455989Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}